## Context

Keep this evidence preparation before the migration, but remove the false claim that one new commit destroys retained Git history. A later log query can start from the preserved pre-migration revision. First Git appearance is not necessarily real creation, and last touch is not necessarily semantic freshness.

Design baseline: v7. v6 was merged by PR #903 and is frozen at `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-v6.md`; v7 closes the acceptance review's four §12.1 conditions and settles the nomenclature, leaving the architecture and this ticket's scope unchanged. See `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`, `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` and `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md`. This planning change implements nothing and closes no work.

## Relevant files

`memory/.provenance.json`; existing `projects/*/memory/` and `memory/` bodies; Git history at the captured baseline.

## Actions

1. Record the baseline commit and per-body original path/blob identity before rewriting or moving files.
2. Extract first observable Git appearance and last relevant touch, recording meaning and uncertainty. Preserve existing first_seen/last_confirmed separately.
3. List undatable bodies, imports and renames requiring qualification. Specify how shallow consumers use exported provenance without needing full history.

## Test

Create a fixture repository with initial content, later edit and mechanical migration. Verify dates can still be queried at the baseline after migration; distinguish absent history from zero age.

## Verification

- [ ] Run the focused fixtures and show they detect the relevant failure.
- [ ] Run applicable repository checks; record commands, results and limitations.
- [ ] Confirm the implementation matches this scope and v7, including explicit capability limits.

## Invariants

- Canonical bodies and provenance remain reviewable; memory cannot override decisions.
- No mandatory residency, automatic generalisation, new native-memory modes or policy duplication.
- Use the common library and normal Git integration; no runtime implementation is part of the planning PR.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Baseline revision and mapping are durable, documented and reproducible.
- [ ] Dates retain their actual meaning; uncertain or absent dates are reported.
- [ ] No body text is rewritten and no claim of irreversible history loss remains.
